{# The Actions cell of an exception row — the one or two things a person can actually DO about it.

   Plain links in `.row-actions` with core's `.table-action`, the way /admin/bundles/procurement/rfqs
   renders a single row action, rather than the `.row-action-menu` dropdown the document grids use:
   that dropdown is opened by app.js and this application's baseline is that it works without
   JavaScript. A dropdown is worth the trade when it collects six actions; it is not worth it to hide
   one link behind a button that does nothing with scripting off.

   An em dash when there is nothing: every move on this screen is gated on the state that would
   refuse it, so a row whose order is cancelled and whose bill is paid genuinely has nothing left to
   offer. Saying so is the honest answer — inventing a control that would be refused on the next
   screen is the defect item 47 exists to fix. #}
<td data-label="Actions" class="row-actions">
    {% for move in moves %}
        <a class="table-action" href="{{ move.href }}">{{ move.label }}</a>
    {% else %}
        &mdash;
    {% endfor %}
</td>
